│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ merge_ry_params_remove_cyclic_crx                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ This change implements two complementary improvements:                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Merge initial RY parameters**: Unify "ry_even" and "ry_odd" into a single "ry"       │
│                          │ parameter, reducing distinct parameters from 6 to 5. This extreme parameter sharing has   │
│                          │ been shown in literature to improve generalization on small datasets by reducing          │
│                          │ overfitting risk. With fewer degrees of freedom, the model is forced to learn more robust │
│                          │ features that generalize better to harder samples (improving worst_group_margin).         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Remove cyclic CRX bridge**: Remove the CRX gate pair [7, 0] that closes the cycle.   │
│                          │ This gate adds architectural complexity without corresponding benefit - it's the only CRX │
│                          │ acting on qubit 7 and 0, breaking the pattern. Prior high-performing versions (0.68       │
│                          │ score) didn't have this cyclic closure. Removing it reduces circuit depth and gate count  │
│                          │ while maintaining expressivity through the remaining XX, YY, and CRZ entanglement.        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Together, these changes drive toward the observation that leaner ansätze with extreme     │
│                          │ parameter sharing achieved better results. The combination should improve:                │
│                          │ - Parameter efficiency (5 vs 6 distinct parameters)                                       │
│                          │ - Worst-group margin through forced generalization                                        │
│                          │ - Convergence speed through simpler circuit                                               │
│                          │ - Overall combined score back toward 0.68+                                                │
